### Zone 1 — Exterior (shophouse setting)

**Image 1**

```
ARCHITECTURAL CONTEXT:

  Building type: Conservation shophouse (two-storey)
  Facade:        Pale teal with red decorative accents
  Setting:       Little India, adjacent to other
                 heritage shophouses in a conservation
                 precinct

  This is the ONLY temple in this corpus housed in a
  shophouse rather than a purpose-built temple structure.
  All previously analyzed temples occupy dedicated
  temple buildings; this institution occupies a domestic/
  commercial building type converted to religious use.

  The shophouse setting is itself a historical record:
  it reflects the institutional circumstances of a
  sect that could not acquire dedicated temple land
  during periods of ban and stigmatization. The
  zhaitang was sustained within an ordinary urban
  building to remain unobtrusive.
```

**Entrance architecture:**

```
Arch plaque:       观音宫 (Guānyīn Gōng)
                   — "Guanyin Palace"
                   — three characters in decorative
                      arch format above the entrance

Red metal gate:    Ornate ironwork gate marking the
                   threshold between street and
                   sacred interior

Red brick pillars: Flanking the entrance on both sides,
                   providing visual separation from
                   adjacent shophouses

Visible interior:  A colorful deity statue visible
                   through the gate from the street
                   (possibly a smaller threshold figure)

Guardian pairs:    No dedicated stone guardian lions
                   visible (consistent with shophouse
                   scale — no space for them). The gate
                   itself serves the threshold-marking
                   function.
```

**Threshold note:**

The gate and arch constitute the threshold programme. Unlike large compound temples (LSSL, KMSPKS) with elaborate mountain gates, sutra pillars, and approach sequences, or urban temples (Kwan Im Thong Hood Cho, Shuntian Gong) with ceremonial pailou gateways, the zhaitang threshold is reduced to a single arch-gate unit — the minimum architecturally sufficient to mark the transition from public street to sacred interior.

```
ENSEMBLE (entrance) →
  [ARCH_PLAQUE  identifies  GUANYIN_GONG]
    [scope: compound]
  [GATE  marks  THRESHOLD]
    [scope: compound]
  [SHOPHOUSE_FORM  manifests  INSTITUTIONAL_CONCEALMENT]
    [scope: complex]
  -- the domestic building form was itself a form of
     institutional protection during the period of ban
```

