### Syncretic mode — fourth institutional religion type

Xiantiandao adds a fourth religious tradition to the corpus's syncretic spectrum:

```
XIANTIANDAO (先天道) — Way of Former Heaven
  Doctrinal synthesis: Confucianism + Buddhism + Daoism
    with millenarian eschatology and supreme female deity
  Core practice: vegetarianism, neidan, spirit-writing
  Institutional form: zhaitang managed by female
                      practitioners (斋姑)
  Register: salvationist sectarian tradition
            — distinct from mainstream Buddhism,
              Taoism, and folk religion

Position in RFIG syncretic taxonomy:

  Mode 1: Architectural Separation (LSSL)
  Mode 2: Iconographic Integration (Da Feng Gu Si)
  Mode 3: Spatial Integration at Compound Scope
           (Kwan Im Thong Hood Cho)
  Mode 4: Architectural Differentiation with Interior
           Permeability (Loyang Tua Pek Kong)

  Mode 5 — Doctrinal Layering (先天道斋堂观音宫):
           A SINGLE institution carries multiple religious
           identities at different disclosure layers:
           outer/public (Guanyin devotional temple) and
           inner/institutional (Xiantiandao zhaitang
           with Wusheng Laomu at the doctrinal center).
           Syncretism operates through LAYERED IDENTITY
           rather than through spatial, architectural,
           or iconographic arrangement.
```

Mode 5 is the most distinctly different from all previous modes. Modes 1-4 all arrange syncretic elements spatially or architecturally — you can walk through different zones and encounter different traditions. Mode 5 operates through depth of disclosure: what you see (Guanyin temple) and what the institution is (Xiantiandao salvationist community) are distinct layers of the same space. The syncretism is invisible to the uninitiated visitor.

